ibute to uh the police services  and the first responders who have shown  up who despite the challenging  circumstances are are are doing a very  effective job uh showing restraint and  patience uh and showing i think a very  level-headed approach  and for though and what that has done is  it has enabled us to allow parliament to  sit because it is important that  parliamentarians have an opportunity to  debate the emergencies act this is the  first time it has been invoked  we do need to be transparent with  canadians uh from the government's point  of view  the measures that we have introduced are  very targeted they're very circumscribed  to the circumstances  that present us and they have to be  consistent with the charter and we're  very motivated to to giving  all parliamentarians an opportunity to  debate very publicly in the open chamber  those measures so that we can be  informed as to the necessity of the  emergencies act so we can bring about  the end uh the peaceful conclusion to  the illegal blockades  thank you uh i'll make  one last call around to see if anyone  uh has wants to raise their hand for a  question and in the meantime i'll i'll  throw one in i wanted to know about the  the economic risk behind the emergency  we've seen border blockades  um  how how might the government respond  if we see another uh border being border  crossing being blocked after the  emergency order lifts would would  economic damage to canada be enough to  to bring in emergency measures or to  take other strong action  it's an important question i'll say a  few words and then i'll pass to uh  minister lametty and other colleagues  but but certainly the economic impact  that was caused by the blockades at the  ambassador bridge to the tune of nearly  400 million dollars a day uh that idled  jobs that saw plants shut down that  created a devastating negative impact on  the manufacturing sector but not only in  southwestern ontario uh across various  supply chains cannot b